@startuml promotion-defense
' SPEC: _spec/apps/starchess/core/adjutant/strategic/promotion-defense.puml
' purpose: ADJUTANT (SCRIPT STRATEGIC) — the *enemy* promotion race: how a
' visible runner is read (turns, not squares), what counts as an answer, and
' where the tier sits in the ladder.
' covers: packages/starchess-core/src/ai/strategic/runners.ts
' covers: packages/starchess-core/src/ai/strategic/strategies/runners.ts
' covers: packages/starchess-core/src/ai/strategic/context.ts
' covers: packages/starchess-core/src/ai/strategic/strategies/economy.ts
' covers: packages/starchess-core/src/ai/strategic/tracking.ts
' covers: packages/starchess-core/src/ai/strategic/witness.ts
' covers: packages/starchess-core/src/ai/context/marines.ts
' covers: packages/starchess-core/src/__tests__/strategic/runners.test.ts
' covers: packages/starchess-core/src/__tests__/strategic/runnerMemory.test.ts
' covers: packages/starchess-core/src/__tests__/strategic/witness.test.ts
' covers: packages/starchess-arena/src/run/runMatch.ts
' covers: apps/starchess/src/screens/SingleScreen.tsx
' see also: ../weight-ladder.puml (every value, and what a test pins)
' see also: strategic-architecture.puml (where this concern sits in the set)
' see also: ../cc-focus.puml (the same fog-honesty rule, for the CC)
' see also: cc-retaliation.puml (the witness's other reader — the ungated one)
!include ../../../../../themes/proveo.puml

skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultTextAlignment left

title StarChess · Adjutant (SCRIPT STRATEGIC) — promotion defense

package "cross-ply memory (one fold per settled ply)" {
  component "ai/strategic/witness.ts\nwitnessFromEndTurn · runnerCrossings\nthe fog gate on the resolve payload" as WIT <<app>>
  component "ai/strategic/tracking.ts\nseedRunnerMemory · updateRunnersForSnap" as TRACK <<app>>
  component "ai/strategic/runners.ts\nfoldRunnerMemory · advanceGhost\nturn-guarded · one sighting, one ghost" as FOLD <<app>>
  database "RunnerMemory\nghosts: RunnerGhost[]\nourUnits: {at, type}[]\nturn — the repeat-fold guard" as MEM <<db>>
}

package "hosts (thread it, never re-implement it)" {
  component "SingleScreen.tsx\nfoldTracking(snap) for kvs / ccIntel\n+ a settledResolves cursor for the witness" as HOSTA <<host>>
  component "arena run/runMatch.ts\nwitnessFromEndTurn at chip + settle" as HOSTB <<host>>
}

package "intel (once per turn)" {
  component "ai/strategic/runners.ts\nscanEnemyRunners · turnsToPromote\nisUrgentRunner · isSoleBlocker" as SCAN <<app>>
  database "ctx.runners: EnemyRunner[]\nat · steps · turns · path · front\npromoteAt · blockedBy · passed\nseen · confidence · possible" as RUN <<db>>
}

HOSTA ARROW_MAIN WIT : witnessFromEndTurn(esr)
HOSTB ARROW_MAIN WIT : witnessFromEndTurn(esr)
HOSTA ARROW_MAIN TRACK
HOSTB ARROW_MAIN TRACK
WIT ARROW_OPTIONAL FOLD : crossings — the fly-by channel
TRACK ARROW_MAIN FOLD : per side, per ply
FOLD ARROW_MAIN MEM
MEM ARROW_MAIN SCAN : ghosts merged in as unseen runners

package "scoring" {
  component "strategies/runners.ts\nscore(ev) — capture · block · cover · abandon" as SS <<app>>
  component "strategies/economy.ts\nrunnerDeployValue(to) — max, not sum\nRUNNER_GUARD · defending\n· runnerOnDeployRing gates" as SE <<app>>
}

component "ai/strategic/context.ts\nbuildStrategicCtx" as CTX <<app>>
component "__tests__/strategic/runners.test.ts\n12 cases" as TEST <<async>>

CTX ARROW_MAIN SCAN : fog-honest scan of the map
SCAN ARROW_MAIN RUN
CTX ARROW_MAIN RUN : frozen onto the context
RUN ARROW_MAIN SS : one tier per runner
RUN ARROW_MAIN SE : a drop is often the only answer in time
TEST ARROW_MAIN SS
TEST ARROW_MAIN SCAN

note right of SCAN
  **Turns, not squares.** A marine covers **two** ranks in one turn — the gas
  step lands in rank B and its classical in rank C
  (""plans/stacks.ts · marineGasStacks""). The fact itself lives in
  ""ai/context/marines.ts"" as ""RUNNER_RANKS_PER_TURN"" / ""turnsToPromote"", because
  both ""runners.ts"" and ""witness.ts"" need it and either importing the other
  would close a cycle; ""runners.ts"" re-exports both, so no caller moved. So:

  | steps out | turns | tier |
  | 1–2 | 1 | ""RUNNER_STOP_CRITICAL"" 7_000 |
  | 3–4 | 2 | ""RUNNER_STOP_SERIOUS"" 5_500 |
  | 5–6 | 3 | ""RUNNER_STOP_WATCH"" 600 |
  | 7+ | 4+ | not a runner — off the list |

  The **three-squares-out case is the one this module was written for**: it is
  two turns from a BattleShip on our back rank, and by the time it stands two
  squares out the answer already has to be on the board.

  **Fog-honest**, by the same rule as ""cc-focus.puml"": a marine counts only on
  a lit or revealed square. The *hidden* half of the same worry belongs to the
  known-vs-seen ledger (""intel.ts · cheesePromotion""), which answers with a
  guard deploy rather than a blockade on a square we cannot see.

  **Contained ≠ answered, and containment must be *known*.** ""blockedBy"" counts
  a path square only on evidence we are entitled to: **our own unit** there
  (fog or not — our own board is never a guess), **any occupant on a lit or
  revealed square**, or **our own mine** it would walk onto
  (""board/mine-contact.ts""). An enemy unit parked in the dark is **not** a
  blockade: believing in one we cannot see damps the urgency to a quarter and
  is exactly how a runner walks in. This is the module's sharpest fog claim,
  and the reason ""blockedBy"" cannot be a plain ""map.has(s)"".

  A runner that *is* contained keeps ""RUNNER_CONTAINED_SHARE"" 0.25 of its
  tier, because the thing containing it can move.
end note

note as N_ANSWERS
  **The answers, and what each is worth** (share × tier):

  | answer | share | why |
  | capture the runner | ""RUNNER_CAPTURE_SHARE"" 1 | the only permanent one — and only a move whose victim really **is** a marine on ""at"" counts, since a remembered runner's square may hold anything |
  | blockade its ""front"" | ""RUNNER_BLOCK_SHARE"" 0.8 | a marine never shoots forward, so a body stops it dead — but only a mover that **arrives** (""!firesInPlace && move.to === front"") |
  | ...with a sidestep open | ×""RUNNER_SIDESTEP_SHARE"" 0.5 | one of our units on its capture diagonals is a ladder past the blockade |
  | ...blocker hanging | ""RUNNER_HANGING_SHARE"" 0.3 | asked on the POST-move board: enemy attacks ""front"", we do not defend it |
  | cover ""front"" from where we land | ""RUNNER_COVER_SHARE"" 0.35 | it costs the runner a unit, not the race |
  | walk the sole blocker off | −""RUNNER_ABANDON_SHARE"" 0.8 | leaving re-releases it — **unless** the move lands on another path square, or the blocker is fleeing fire |

  **A sieged tank never answers by standing.** It fires without leaving its
  square, so it can shoot a runner but can never *become* the body in front of
  one: the block credit tests ""move.to"", while cover tests the square it
  actually occupies afterwards (""move.from"" when ""firesInPlace""). A classical
  move only scores cover of the **blockade** square; only a **deploy** scores
  an arbitrary square on the path.

  **Fleeing is not abandoning.** When ""tactical.enemyAttacks(move.from)"" and
  ""ev.destSafe()"", the charge is skipped: a blocker the enemy is about to take
  stops being a blocker either way, and survival owns that call. Without the
  exemption the 7_000-point charge pinned a doomed unit to its square.

  The charge is measured on ""runnerStake"" — the tier scaled by confidence, but
  **not** by ""RUNNER_CONTAINED_SHARE"" — rather than on ""runnerUrgency"": the
  urgency is damped *because* our own unit is the blocker, so charging the
  damped number would make walking away nearly free.

  **Deploys** (""strategies/economy.ts"") reuse the shares through
  ""runnerDeployValue"", over every runner on the context, ghosts included — but
  return the **best single answer, not the sum**: one body settles one square,
  so two runners sharing a path can no longer stack a ring square past the tier
  ceiling. A drop onto a runner we can **see** crushes it
  (""board/deploy-reach.ts"" landing rule); onto a *remembered* one it pays only
  ""RUNNER_BLOCK_SHARE"", because that tile is far more often empty than
  occupied. An urgent runner also counts as ""defending"" — no ship-saving spree,
  ""RUNNER_GUARD"" tank on — and, when its square or path touches the ring, buys
  back the light drops ""preferHeavy"" would skip.

  **Ally-occupied ring squares are legal answers too** (the Command Center may
  crush its own non-king units; kings are skipped at generation). The deploy
  loop prices the victim (mirror of the enemy-crush bonus), but the runner
  answer itself pays **nothing** on ally-held squares — the standing body
  already blockades it, so re-buying the square would be pure victim + gas
  (the seen-crush / remembered-block shares above apply to non-ally squares
  only). A square holding some runner's last blocker carries the abandon
  charge and never shields elsewhere (""ALLY_UPGRADE"" /
  ""ALLY_SHIELD_MARINE"" / ""ALLY_SHIELD_SHIP"" in ""strategies/economy.ts"").
end note

note as N_MEMORY
  **Remembering the marine that ran by.** A unit that only *slides through* a
  lit square is gone by the time the next settled snapshot is read, so a scan
  of the board can never see it. The memory is the answer, and it is folded
  exactly like the CC posterior and the known-vs-seen ledger — in core, one
  fold per settled ply, threaded by both hosts (never re-implemented in one).

  **Two ways in.** A ghost is seeded either by a **departure** (a square we can
  see, that we saw a marine on, now empty) or by a **crossing** the witness
  reports (below). Both then advance and reconcile identically.

  **One turn, one propagation — but every ply, its evidence.**
  <color:#CB2000>""beginScTurn"" does **not** advance the clock</color>: a
  turn-open chip ply carries the *same* turn number as the settle before it.
  So ""propagate = snap.turn !== prior.turn"" gates only the forward walk
  (""reach"" 0 instead of ""RUNNER_RANKS_PER_TURN"") and the confidence decay.
  Sightings, crossings and pruning run on **every** ply.

  An earlier draft returned ""prior"" untouched on a repeated turn. That did not
  deduplicate anything — it discarded the chip ply's entire account of itself,
  and a turn-open chip is the most common way a Command Center is ever shot, so
  it silently killed the retaliation channel's main path
  (""cc-retaliation.puml""). An empty memory still starts at turn **−1**, so the
  first fold of turn 0 propagates.

  The fold asks ""getSideVision"" with the **same options the planner uses**
  (""{ mines, tankModeFor: sieged ? "siege" : "tank" }"", per
  ""ai/context/vision.ts""), so a marine our own siege cone is looking at cannot
  be forgotten by a fold that asked for less light.

  A ""RunnerGhost"" is ""{ seenAt, seenTurn, possible[], confidence }"". Per ply,
  ""advanceGhost"" takes each square it could be on and adds: **stood still**,
  **+1 rank**, **+2 ranks** (the gas stack) — never through a visible occupant,
  never through one of ours whether we can see it or not, and never *past* one
  of our own mines, which kill a marine that walks onto them — plus any
  **forward diagonal where one of ours was actually lost**. Then it prunes:

  | evidence | effect |
  | a lit square with no marine on it | that square is out — this is what makes a *departure* legible |
  | a visible occupant in the way | the walk stops there |
  | one of our own units on the square | out, fog or no fog — our own board is never a guess |
  | our own mine on the walk | the walk stops there, for the same reason |
  | the promotion rank | dropped — it is a BattleShip now, the ledger's problem |
  | a sighting this ghost could have reached | the ghost **is** that marine and retires into the fresh sighting — **at most one ghost per sighting** |
  | a sighting some *other* ghost already absorbed | not a match: this ghost merely learns somebody else stands there, and prunes that square |
  | nothing left in the set | dropped: captured, promoted, or never there |

  The one-to-one rule is what keeps two records from describing one marine.
  Both entry paths go through the one ""carry()"" helper, so a **crossing also
  supersedes any ghost whose set it overlaps** — it is the same marine, seen
  again. Deduplicating a crossing against settled sightings alone left last
  turn's ghost *and* this turn's crossing on the books, doubling both the tier
  and the deploy value. Its read-side twin is in ""scanEnemyRunners"": a ghost is
  placed on its first **unclaimed** possible square rather than dropped when
  its worst case is already spoken for.

  Confidence starts at 1 and is multiplied by ""RUNNER_MEMORY_DECAY"" 0.7 each
  silent ply, dropping through ""RUNNER_MEMORY_FLOOR"" 0.2 after ~5 — and it
  **scales every share** (""runnerStake""), so a remembered runner is always
  worth less than a seen one and a faint one is worth almost nothing.
  ""RUNNER_URGENT_CONFIDENCE"" 0.5 is the bar a ghost must clear before it may
  cancel the economy's plans or damp the seek dial.

  **The one thing credited without vision** is our own loss — and it is a
  *loss*, not a departure. ""RunnerMemory.ourUnits"" holds our previous squares
  **with their types**, and a vacated square counts as a capture diagonal only
  when that type's count actually dropped between folds. A knight that merely
  hopped off a runner's diagonal leaves its square empty too; crediting that as
  a capture invented an advance the enemy never made, and the invented square
  is the most advanced one, so it became the ghost's head and drove the tier.
  Widening the set is **not** automatically safe: the head of the set is what
  the scan reads.
end note

note as N_SEEK
  **Why the seek dial is damped, not out-bid.** ""seekFocus"" is the one posture
  weight that *multiplies*: ""mapControl"" pays it per square closed toward the
  CC guess, times a cue of up to 3, so a tank strolling four squares down the
  board banks more than any flat weight in the ladder. Rather than inflate a
  constant until it wins that race, ""buildStrategicCtx"" damps the dial to
  ""RUNNER_SEEK_DAMP"" 0.25 while an urgent runner is on the board — searching
  for a Command Center we may not find this turn is precisely the concern that
  can wait. The damp is a **multiplier** on the posture value, not a
  replacement: ""seek"" 700 becomes 175, ""expand"" and ""hunt"" are already 0. It
  lives in the **context**, so strategies keep reading ""ctx.weights"" and never
  branch on the runner themselves.
end note

note as N_WITNESS
  <color:#1A7F37>**CLOSED — the fly-by is now readable (layer B).**</color> A
  marine that crosses a lit square mid-turn and lands in fog leaves no trace on
  any two settled boards. The resolve knows, so the resolve is asked — through
  a gate, because the payload is emitted with **no viewer**, and handing it
  over raw is the omniscience ""../cc-focus.puml"" forbids.

  ""ai/strategic/witness.ts"" is that gate. Hosts hand in a ""ResolveWitness""
  (""{ placement, mines, siegedTanks, moves, attacks }""), never a raw engine
  payload, and there is exactly **one** adapter — ""witnessFromEndTurn"" — because
  a turn-open chip ply also builds a whole ""EndScTurnResult"".

  **It carries the whole ply, not the classical slice.** ""moves"" is
  ""bMoves + resolved.moves + fMoves"": a marine that advances by **gas step
  alone** crosses our light exactly like a classical mover, and dropping the B
  phase blinded the channel to half of the two-ranks-a-turn advance it exists
  to catch. ""attacks"" likewise adds ""moveCollisions"" and both mine-trigger
  lists — the latter is how we learn our own mine did the work.

  ""runnerCrossings(witness, side)"" gates every enemy marine move:

  | step | rule |
  | whose fog | read from ""esr.afterB.placement"" — the board we were watching **from** when the turn began, with the planner's own ""{ mines, tankModeFor }"" bag |
  | what counts | any square of ""[from, ...rayBetween(from,to), to]"" that was lit or revealed |
  | the origin, extra | granted when ""isMoverVisibleToSide"" says firing gave it away — a shot's origin is ""stopAt ?? from"", because a slider that discovers its target mid-ray fires from the discovery square and comes to **rest** elsewhere |
  | what is reported | the **most advanced** square we actually watched it occupy, plus a ""residual"" — never where the move ended |

  **The residual is the correction that keeps the ghost honest.** The move is
  already over when we fold, so advancing a crossing a full turn would place
  the marine a rank beyond anywhere it could be. ""residual =
  RUNNER_RANKS_PER_TURN − travelled"", where ""travelled"" is ""dist(origin, at)""
  when we watched it leave, and **1** otherwise — an unwatched origin means
  ""at"" is a square it passed *through*, so a rank is already spent. Nothing
  here consults ""move.to"": that is the payload's knowledge, not ours.

  ""foldRunnerMemory"" seeds a ghost at the crossing square, walks it by exactly
  that residual, and reconciles it like any other ghost. Two further prunes
  apply on the way in: ""ourMarineKills"" — our own shot or our own mine, both
  ours to know — drops a marine we destroyed this ply rather than remembering
  it at large, and the crossing supersedes any ghost it overlaps (above). A
  marine that flew through our light and landed in the dark is therefore
  remembered, which is the case this note used to list as missing.

  **"Firing reveals the shooter" is a rule this slice asserts, not one it
  borrows.** ""board/vision.ts isMoverVisibleToSide"" existed but had no caller
  outside the barrel; this channel is now its only one. So the precedent is
  ours to keep consistent — if the UI ever grows a second reveal gate, these
  two must agree or the script and the board will disagree about what a player
  was allowed to see.

  The same witness carries the *other* honest channel, a shot on our Command
  Center, which needs no gate at all: ""strategic/cc-retaliation.puml"".
end note

note as N_CADENCE
  **Two cadences in the app host, and the witness gets the better one.**
  ""foldTracking"" folds ""kvs"" and ""ccIntel"" off the *planning* snapshot, guarded
  by ""foldedTurn"" — an approximation, since a turn is not a ply.

  The witness folds run off ""store.shell().settledResolves"", which the reducer
  appends to exactly once per settled ply (""matchReducer.ts:549"" and "":769""),
  chip plies included — the same two points the arena folds at. A
  ""foldedResolves"" cursor drains it, so a re-render can never fold a ply twice.
  That cursor is the only thing preventing a double fold: the fold's own
  ""propagate"" test is a per-*turn* guard and two plies of one turn both count.

  Two placement rules, both load-bearing:
  * the effect is declared **above** ""<BaseScreen>"", so it is created first and
    runs before the effect that asks the Adjutant to plan — otherwise the
    opponent plans one ply behind its own memory;
  * it advances the cursor and returns when the opponent is not the strategic
    script. Nothing reads these memories under naive, and folding them would be
    two vision passes per side per ply for no reader.

  <color:#CB2000>**OPEN — a resumed match starts blind.**</color> The resume
  path rebuilds state from a single SCFEN, so ""settledResolves"" is empty and
  all four memories come back at the opening prior: the ghosts, the wanted
  list, the posterior and the ledger are gone. Nothing in the blob carries
  them, and a resumed game is a stated goal of this branch. **Not fixed.**
end note

note as N_NONGOALS
  **Deliberate non-goals.**
  - **King moves score 0 here.** The Command Center stepping in front of a
    runner is survival's call; this concern never bids the CC into a blockade.
  - **No candidate generation.** Every answer is either a classical move the
    orchestrator already generates or a deploy ""economy"" already proposes.
  - **Stacks are scored by the naive ""scoreMove""** (""plans/stacks.ts"" imports
    ""../strategies""), so a runner answer that only exists as a B+C stack does
    not see these weights. Pre-existing, and unchanged here.
  - **Our own promotion race is untouched** — ""NEAR_PROMOTE_RUSH"" in
    ""ai/strategies/mapControl.ts"" still reads only *our* marines, and still
    only within 2 steps.
end note

SCAN .. N_ANSWERS
MEM .. N_MEMORY
CTX .. N_SEEK
SS .. N_NONGOALS
HOSTA .. N_CADENCE
FOLD .. N_WITNESS

note bottom of TEST
  ""__tests__/strategic/runnerMemory.test.ts"" pins the fold in **11 cases**: a
  marine kept after it steps out of sight (bounded by two ranks), the ghost
  read at its worst-case square, collapse back to a sighting, the decay to
  nothing, capture credit through fog, **a unit that merely moved crediting
  nothing**, **the chip-ply guard** (the same turn folded twice changes
  nothing), **two ghosts and one marine** (one absorbs, one survives),
  promotion dropping the ghost, a guard deploy driven by memory alone — and
  that a marine we *never saw* leaves no ghost however many plies it walks.

  ""__tests__/strategic/runners.test.ts"" pins **12 cases**: the turns table and
  the watch horizon, contained-vs-passed, the capture / block / abandon scores,
  a whole ""fog honesty"" describe (an enemy blocker in the dark does **not**
  contain the runner, while a *seen* blocker and any unit of ours do), and two
  end-to-end plans — taking a runner two turns out instead of aiming at the CC
  guess, and deploying a blocker on ""f7"". That fixture is deliberate: ""f7"" is
  the **worst** ring square by ordinary deploy scoring (the centre pull favours
  ""d7"" / ""e7""), and the same board with the runner removed must **not** drop
  there — the control that fails if the deploy term is deleted. The tier
  ordering itself is pinned in ""__tests__/tactics/weightOrdering.test.ts""
  (see ""../weight-ladder.puml"").

  ""__tests__/strategic/witness.test.ts"" pins the gate in **8 cases**, seven of
  them driven through the real engine rather than hand-built payloads: a
  genuine double-step settled through ""endScTurn"" reporting the furthest square
  we watched **and its residual**, the per-viewer gate (the mover's own side
  learns nothing), a path entirely in the dark reporting nothing at all, the
  memory getting a ghost the settled board alone could not produce — and four
  retaliation cases (""cc-retaliation.puml""), one of which asserts
  ""chip.next.turn === settle.next.turn"" inline so the clock trap stays visible
  to the next reader.

  ""runnerMemory.test.ts"" holds the fold's own version of that trap, now named
  "advances once per turn, not once per ply".
end note
@enduml
